/Title (A Common Pipeline for Text Clustering)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 532 0 R
/First 537 0 R
/Last 538 0 R
/Count -4
/Next 539 0 R
>>
endobj
536 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 540 0 R
/Next 533 0 R
>>
endobj
537 0 obj
<<
/Title (Text clustering allows for discovering patterns in data that you may or may not be familiar with. It allows for getting an intuitive understanding of the task, for example, a classification task, but also of its complexity. As a result, text clustering can become more than just a quick method for exploratory data analysis. Although there are many methods for text clustering, from graph-based neural net works to centroid-based clustering techniques, a common pipeline that has gained popularity involves three steps and algorithms:)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 535 0 R
/Next 541 0 R
>>
endobj
538 0 obj
<<
/Title (3. Find groups of semantically similar documents with a cluster model.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 535 0 R
/Prev 542 0 R
>>
endobj
539 0 obj
<<
/Title (Embedding Documents)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 535 0 R
/First 543 0 R
/Last 544 0 R
/Count -2
/Next 545 0 R
>>
endobj
540 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 546 0 R
/Next 536 0 R
>>
endobj
541 0 obj
<<
/Title (1. Convert the input documents to embeddings with an embedding model.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 535 0 R
/Prev 537 0 R
/Next 542 0 R
>>
endobj
542 0 obj
<<
/Title (2. Reduce the dimensionality of embeddings with a dimensionality reduction model.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 535 0 R
/Prev 541 0 R
/Next 538 0 R
>>
endobj
543 0 obj
<<
/Title (The first step is to convert our textual data to embeddings, as illustrated in Figure 5-3. Recall from previous chapters that embeddings are numerical representations of text that attempt to capture its meaning.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 539 0 R
/Next 544 0 R
>>
endobj
544 0 obj
<<
/Title (Choosing embedding models optimized for semantic similarity tasks is especially important for clustering as we attempt to find groups of semantically similar documents. Fortunately, most embedding models at the time of writing focus on just that, semantic similarity)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 539 0 R
/Prev 543 0 R
>>
endobj
545 0 obj
<<
/Title (Reducing the Dimensionality of Embeddings)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 539 0 R
/First 547 0 R
/Last 548 0 R
/Count -2
/Next 549 0 R
>>
endobj
546 0 obj
<<
/Title (From Text Clustering to Topic Modeling)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 550 0 R
/First 551 0 R
/Last 552 0 R
/Count -2
/Next 540 0 R
>>
endobj
547 0 obj
<<
/Title (Before we cluster the embeddings, we will first need to take their high dimensionality into account. As the number of dimensions increases, there is an exponential growth in the number of possible values within each dimension. Finding all subspaces within each dimension becomes increasingly complex. As a result, high-dimensional data can be troublesome for many clustering tech niques as it gets more difficult to identify meaningful clusters. Instead, we can make use of dimensionality reduction.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 545 0 R
/Next 548 0 R
>>
endobj
548 0 obj
<<
/Title (As illustrated in Figure 5-4, this technique allows us to reduce the size of the dimensional space and represent the same data with fewer dimensions. Dimensionality reduction techniques aim to preserve the global structure of high-dimensional data by finding low-dimensional representations.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 545 0 R
/Prev 547 0 R
>>
endobj
549 0 obj
<<
/Title (Note that this is a compression technique and that the underlying algorithm is not arbitrarily removing dimensions. To help the cluster model create meaningful clus ters, the second step in our clustering pipeline is therefore dimensionality reduction, as shown in Figure 5-5.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 545 0 R
/Next 550 0 R
>>
endobj
550 0 obj
<<
/Title (Cluster the Reduced Embeddings)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 549 0 R
/First 553 0 R
/Last 554 0 R
/Count -2
/Next 546 0 R
>>
endobj
551 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 546 0 R
/Next 552 0 R
>>
endobj
552 0 obj
<<
/Title (This idea of finding themes or latent topics in a collection of textual data is often referred to as topic modeling. Traditionally, it involves finding a set of keywords or phrases that best represent and capture the meaning of the topic, as we illustrate in Figure 5-9)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 546 0 R
/Prev 551 0 R
>>
endobj
553 0 obj
<<
/Title (The third step is to cluster the reduced embeddings, as illustrated in Figure 5-6)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 550 0 R
/Next 554 0 R
>>
endobj
554 0 obj
<<
/Title (Although a common choice is a centroid-based algorithm like k-means, which requires a set of clusters to be generated, we do not know the number of clusters beforehand. Instead, a density-based algorithm freely calculates the number of clus ters and does not force all data points to be part of a cluster, as illustrated in Figure 5-7.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 550 0 R
/Prev 553 0 R
>>
endobj
